So I drew some cans of cola
here to kind of try to emphasize

00:16:55.319 --> 00:16:56.870
exactly what this is all about.

00:16:57.399 --> 00:17:01.409
So let's say if you have a soda can or
soda bottle, and if you're not opening

00:17:01.410 --> 00:17:05.930
it, one thing that's really inside of that
mall inside of there is there is if you

00:17:05.930 --> 00:17:09.290
were to zoom in on the, on the molecular
level, you will see that there's going

00:17:09.290 --> 00:17:12.490
to be CO2 dissolved within the solution.

00:17:12.990 --> 00:17:18.165
And that's because there's so much CO2 in
the, in the space above it, That's going

00:17:18.165 --> 00:17:21.525
to be pushing all the gases into solution.

00:17:22.295 --> 00:17:28.324
Now, when all of a sudden you open up
the can and it goes, when it opens,

00:17:28.324 --> 00:17:32.395
right, that standard sound that you've
heard all heard before, all the CO2

00:17:32.395 --> 00:17:36.465
is going to escape because it's going
from an area of a really small volume.

00:17:36.870 --> 00:17:39.170
to the open space, a big volume.

00:17:39.700 --> 00:17:42.760
Now, when that happens, well, all
the gas molecules that are bouncing

00:17:42.760 --> 00:17:47.160
around, they, now, there's not
going to be so many of them to try

00:17:47.160 --> 00:17:50.040
to keep, uh, CO2 in the liquid.

00:17:50.325 --> 00:17:53.545
Therefore, your soda is
going to go flat over time.

00:17:54.085 --> 00:17:56.955
So one thing to kind of keep in
mind is, well, how do you keep

00:17:56.965 --> 00:17:59.014
preserved soda for a really long time?

00:17:59.365 --> 00:18:02.784
Well, one is don't open it as many
